### 4\. Important implementation advice

There are three things I particularly want you to get right:

* Don't compromise authentication: Everyone should be able to register, but nobody should be able to register as an admin.

* Don't compromise scientific accuracy: Actual DNA matching must use reference databases, not AI-generated organism names.

* Don't compromise data security: Firebase and Supabase must enforce permissions on the backend, not merely through frontend navigation.

Also, Google AI Studio may generate the frontend and initial backend integration, but you will still need to configure Firebase, Supabase, API credentials, database policies and deployment.

### 5\. Recommended final application structure

![WildMon](https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/TePGSRDh4Lp8SQ_Lu6q6U8rz1uODWW3WB_HaQsLDUFBZyds1rCQLgGgeXyogg5rPiFmB0FeSMYejbsKINyfukqgQMRfZcOknQ2XXQhNpuFTTUb-FBLgicKAJXvJWSwZ_FLtyCsYuVIwj91yRYDyBbk6ptAsCRI1tzv_qrU9kQ1NWxeiO33JG34xzXABCBZdJ?purpose=fullsize)

Your final application should have three distinct experiences:

* Public: Landing page, registration, login and project information.

* Registered users: Personal research dashboard, dataset analysis, species identification and reports.

* Administrators: Hidden `/control-center` environment with user management, database monitoring, analysis monitoring and audit logs.

My recommendation: Build the authentication and database integration first, verify that they work, and only then develop the scientific analysis modules. This will save you considerable debugging time.

